@nonumber
# Honorifics in interaction

A proposal for comparative reading

This sample essay develops questions for reading about honorifics. Its references have verified bibliographic records; the essay does not claim to summarize the findings of works whose full texts have not been examined. References below identify reading topics suggested by titles. The proposed distinctions and examples are the present writer's own, and no empirical study is reported here.

@nonumber
## From a form to a question

An honorific expression can be recorded as a linguistic form. Explaining its use requires a further decision about what the explanation should cover. A comparison might concern the available forms, the speaker's choice, the recipient's response, or the analyst's interpretation. I propose keeping these questions separate at the start of a reading project, even when a later account brings them together.

Agha's article is titled “Honorification,” providing a broad entry point for the proposed reading [cite agha1994]. Okamoto's title explicitly combines situated politeness with honorific and non-honorific expressions in Japanese conversations [cite okamoto1999]. These titles motivate different scales of inquiry. They do not, by themselves, establish what either author concludes about the relation between a form and its context.

Consider two invented requests: “Could you send the file?” and “Send the file, please.” These are illustrative English sentences, not transcripts and not examples taken from the cited works. Before comparing their evaluations, a study would need to specify who addresses whom, what has already happened, and how a response is recorded. The sentences alone cannot supply those observations. This simple limitation is useful when planning what evidence a comparison should collect.

@nonumber
## What is being compared?

Brown and Levinson's book places universals in its title [cite brown-levinson1987]. Matsumoto's title announces a reexamination of the universality of face, while Ide's identifies formal forms and discernment [cite matsumoto1988 ide1989]. A responsible comparison of these works must first establish the object of each argument from the text. Dividing them into opposing camps on the basis of their titles would skip precisely the reading that is needed.

For that reading, I would prepare three columns: the proposed object of explanation, the evidence offered, and the scope claimed for the result. A fourth column would record unresolved terms. The point is not to turn different arguments into identical ones. It is to make the basis of a comparison explicit, including cases where the same word does different work in different accounts.

Pizziconi's title brings politeness, face, and the Japanese language into a reexamination [cite pizziconi2003]. Fukada and Asato's title names an application of universal politeness theory to Japanese honorifics [cite fukada-asato2004]. These records make both works relevant to a reading plan organized around comparison. Whether their definitions, evidence, or conclusions agree remains a question for examination of the works themselves.

@nonumber
## A sequence rather than a verdict

The bibliographic record for Haugh's article names a discursive challenge and an interactional alternative [cite haugh2007]. This title prompts a methodological question for the present proposal: what would be lost if a sequence were reduced to a single evaluation of one utterance? I would retain the order of turns and distinguish a participant's displayed response from an analyst's later description. This is a proposal for recording evidence, not a claim about the procedure used in that article.

The same caution applies to disagreement. If two analysts evaluate an expression differently, their disagreement need not be treated as an inconvenient remainder. One can ask whether they had the same contextual information, applied the same terms, and judged the same aspect of the exchange. The answers would determine what a comparison can support. A measure of agreement without these distinctions may describe less than its name suggests.

Watts's book and Kádár and Haugh's book provide two further entries in the reading plan [cite watts2003 kadar-haugh2013]. Their titles, “Politeness” and “Understanding politeness,” are broad. I would use them to test whether the distinctions in the proposed reading table remain useful across a larger discussion. A broad title is a reason to inspect scope carefully, not permission to attribute a general position to the author.

@nonumber
## Cultural specificity as a research question

Pizziconi's chapter title explicitly relates honorifics, cultural specificity, and a universal mechanism in Japanese [cite pizziconi2011]. The relation is therefore a suitable question to bring to the reading table. It would be premature to treat the terms as either compatible or incompatible before examining how they are defined and connected in the chapter.

My provisional approach is to compare both the categories used to describe expressions and the situations in which those categories are applied. Matching a category across languages does not settle whether its use is comparable. Conversely, different category names do not establish that no comparison is possible. Each proposed match should carry a statement of what is shared, what differs, and what has not been checked.

This brings the project back to situated choices [cite okamoto1999] and to the discursive question identified earlier [cite haugh2007]. The aim is not a single verdict that an expression is polite in every relevant sense. It is an account whose steps can be examined: the available evidence, the unit selected for analysis, the interpretation made, and the limits retained. Full-text reading and an explicitly designed empirical study would be the next stages; this essay supplies neither in advance.

